{{#if skills}}
- The Skill Documents at the top are the grounding truth of this plan: follow them as authoritative instructions, not as background reading.
{{/if}}
{{#if writing_styles}}
- The Writing styles References&Instructions above tell you how this unit's prose must read: follow the diction, rhythm, and imagery they show when you plan its beats, so the plan carries those choices forward rather than a neutral default.
{{/if}}
{{#if skills}}
- When you draft a unit's own `writing_styles` entries, draw them from the Skill Documents: name the register and the exact words to use, and quote the example structures that fit that unit's beat. State diction and technique. Numbers: an amount the outline or an in-force constraint fixes keeps its own value — derive the unit-scaled instance instead of replacing it with a band quoted from the references (the unit's word budget × the ratio → a dialogue quota in characters or lines); where no such figure exists, cite the skill documents' own calibrated band instead of inventing a number.
{{else}}
- When you draft a unit's own `writing_styles` entries, state the diction and technique its prose must use: name the register and, where they matter, the exact words. An amount the outline or an in-force constraint fixes keeps its own value and is derived to this unit's scale; never invent other amounts.
{{/if}}
{{#if writing_constraints}}
- The Writing Constrains above are binding, not advisory: every unit you plan must respect them — never propose a beat, event, or guidance that breaks one.
{{/if}}
- Plan only the unit(s) the Goal asks for: every unit you propose must stay inside the {{ parent_title }}'s Title and Description above and must not stage events that belong to another unit.
- A unit carries only its own share of the {{ parent_title }}: never re-plan or summarise the whole arc inside one unit, and let the units you propose together cover the Description exactly once — one beat belongs to one unit only. A unit starts exactly where the unit before it stops: it must not re-stage that unit's closing beat — the pose, the state or the action it ends on — or any moment already covered, and opens with the first beat after it. The first unit starts at the Description's own first beat: no setup, recap or arrival before it.
- The writing constraints you attach to a unit bind that unit alone and reach the prose prompt with it: write what that unit adds, tightens or overrides — never restate a rule already in force above, it reaches the prose on its own, and never write an event order spanning other units.
- Hard requirements carry forward as derived numbers: for every hard number, `must` or `must not` that the outline or a constraint above fixes and this unit must honor — above all the measurable shape of the prose, such as dialogue share, mandatory marks, required word families, or counts that must not be exceeded — attach the unit-scaled countable instance (the unit's word budget × the ratio → a dialogue quota in characters or lines; a required-word floor from a named family) as one of this unit's own constraints — the one allowed exception to the no-restating rule above. The unit's own clauses are the last ones in the prose prompt's constraint list, and the prose prompt resolves a conflict in favour of the later clause — so these derived values are what the writer measures against; dropping or weakening one is a failed plan.

